```

The method IndexOf(...) method of ArrayList has two preconditions: 
- That x is in the array (to test its behavior)
- That x is not in the array (to test a failure case for it)  

Also, the method IndexOf(...) has three postconditions: 
- New_arrList_Count equals old_arrList_Count + the number of additional items you want in the array.
- New_arrList_LastIndexOf_x equals old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x - the number of items you removed from the array 
  plus the number of items you added to the array.
- New_Ret equals the old index of x in the old array plus the number of additional items you want 
in the array minus the number of items you removed from the array. 

Similarly, 
- New_arrList_contains_x is the same as the old value of arrList contains x after performing a state preservation.

If these conditions are maintained while writing this method you can test any contract that your ArrayList class should follow. 

Remember, you should always use specific values for the inputs, and always provide different and meaningful values as inputs to satisfy your precondition and to test the expected functioning of the SUT (System Under Test). Always ensure your test data contains the expected behaviour for the array operations.

## Tips and Tricks

- Test the code in various scenarios.
- Use the various collection methods.
- Verify the effects on various array sizes (e.g., adding and removing items in an empty array). 
- Test with more or less items than the physical maximum array size supported by the system.
